<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 2.3 Taylor Series and its convergence.

  Covers 2.3.1-2.3.5 of the full lecture notes (Taylor.ptx).  In 2.3.4
  only the first part, Computing Limits and Integrals with Taylor Series,
  is examinable; the three physics applications (relativistic kinetic
  energy, the pendulum, approximating pi) are starred, exactly as in the
  full notes.

  Theorems, definitions, and problem statements are kept; derivations
  and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes with a one-line
  instruction.  Every xml:id is prefixed with "skel-".

  Interactive figures (GeoGebra, Desmos) and the videos are marked
  component="web": publication.ptx keeps them, publication-print.ptx
  drops them, so they appear on the web but not in the PDF.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-series-taylor-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Taylor Series and its convergence</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
The main objectives of this section are to answer the following questions:
</p>
</introduction>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Under what conditions is the function <m>f(x)</m> equal to the Taylor
series generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>? That is, when can we say
<m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m>,
<m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k</m>, is used as
an approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, how can we estimate the error in this
approximation? That is, how can we estimate the magnitude of
<m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="thm-skel-taylor-formula"/> and
<xref ref="thm-skel-remainder-theorem"/> will enable us to answer these
questions. We begin by stating Taylor's formula and the remainder
estimation theorem, and then use them to bound the error in a Taylor
approximation, discuss some applications of these theorems, and close
with the proof of the remainder theorem.
</p>
<p>
<em>These are skeletal notes: the theorems and problem statements are
given, and blank boxes are left wherever a derivation or a solution
belongs, so that you can fill them in by hand.</em>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-taylor-formula">
<title>Taylor's Formula and The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-taylor-formula">
<title>Taylor's Formula</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on
an open interval <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each
<m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a
number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-formula-eq" number="yes">
f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
</md>
where
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-polynomial-eq" number="yes">
p_n(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
</md>
and
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-remainder" number="yes">
R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-taylor-formula">
<caption>Taylor's Formula: <m>f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x)</m>. The Taylor
polynomial <m>p_n</m> agrees with <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>; away from
<m>a</m>, the vertical gap between them is the remainder
<m>R_n(x)</m>.</caption>
<image width="65%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-taylor-formula">
<diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="8">
<definition>a = 1</definition>
<definition>f(x) = 0.9 + 0.55*sin(1.15*(x - 0.4)) + 0.09*x</definition>
<definition>pn(x) = f(a) + 0.5778*(x - a) - 0.379*(x - a)^2</definition>
<definition>xt = 3.1</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.4, -0.3, 4.1, 2.3)">
<axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
       stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="pngraph" function="pn" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
       stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
<group at="tangency">
<point p="(a, f(a))" fill="black" size="3"/>
<line p1="(a, 0)" p2="(a, f(a))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
<label anchor="(a, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>a</m></label>
</group>
<group at="remainder">
<line p1="(xt, 0)" p2="(xt, f(xt))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
<label anchor="(xt, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>x</m></label>
<point p="(xt, f(xt))" fill="blue" size="3"/>
<point p="(xt, pn(xt))" fill="red" size="3"/>
<line p1="(xt, pn(xt))" p2="(xt, f(xt))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<label anchor="(xt, pn(xt))" alignment="sw" offset="(-8,-14)">
<m>R_n(x)</m>
</label>
</group>
<label at="flabel" anchor="(3.55, f(3.55))" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)">
<m>f(x)</m>
</label>
<label at="pnlabel" anchor="(3.5, pn(3.5))" alignment="e" offset="(6,-2)">
<m>p_n(x)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="fgraph"
    text="A schematic illustration of Taylor's Formula, f of x equals p n of x plus R n of x. A function f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree at x equals a and separate as x moves away, the gap being the remainder">
  <annotation ref="fgraph"
      text="The graph of the function f of x, shown in blue"
      sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="pngraph"
      text="The graph of the Taylor polynomial p n of x, shown in red, which is tangent to f at x equals a and closely matches f near a"
      sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="tangency"
      text="The point x equals a, where f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree; the approximation is exact here"/>
  <annotation ref="remainder"
      text="At a point x away from a, the vertical gap between f and p n is the remainder R n of x, the error in the Taylor approximation"/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p component="web">
For a visual representation of Taylor's formula, watch the animation in
<xref ref="fig-skel-vid-taylor-formula"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="90%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-vid-taylor-formula">
<caption>Taylor's Formula Animation.</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-taylor-formula"
       source="videos/taylor_formula_1080p.mp4"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

<remark xml:id="rmk-skel-error-upper-bound">
<title>Finding an upper bound for the error term without knowing the value of <m>c</m></title>
<p>
Usually the value of <m>c</m> is not explicitly known. However, we may
manage to find <alert>an upper bound</alert> for the error term
<m>R_n(x)</m> without knowing the exact value of <m>c</m>. This is
achieved by finding an upper bound <m>M</m> for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m>,
where <m>c</m> is between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, and then using this
upper bound to estimate the error term.
</p>
<p>
This is the idea behind every error estimate in this section. It is used
in <xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-convergence"/> to prove that the Taylor
series of <m>e^x</m> converges to <m>e^x</m> for every <m>x</m>, and in
each of the three examples of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-error"/>.
</p>
</remark>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-remainder-theorem">
<title>The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on
an open interval <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each
<m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a
number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
<md xml:id="eq-skel-remainder-bound" number="yes">
|R_n(x)| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1},
</md>
where <m>M</m> is an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the
interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
(Derive <xref ref="eq-skel-remainder-bound"/> from
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-remainder"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for deriving the remainder estimation theorem by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
<alert>You are not expected to know the proof of Taylor's formula
itself</alert>, only to understand the statement and its implications.
The proof is worked through in <xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-proof"/>.
</p>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-taylor-convergence">
<title>An example of a Taylor series that converges</title>

<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. The Taylor series generated by
<m>f</m> at <m>x=0</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-exp-series" number="yes">
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n
= \underline{\hspace{12em}},
</md>
and its remainder is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-exp-remainder" number="yes">
R_n(x) = \underline{\hspace{12em}}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
(Compute <xref ref="eq-skel-exp-series"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-exp-remainder"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {Taylor series of $e^x$ at $x = 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$R_n(x) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two halves, for computing the Taylor series of e to the x at zero and its remainder.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
(Show that <xref ref="eq-skel-exp-series"/> converges to <m>e^x</m> for
every <m>x\in\R</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for showing that the Taylor series of e to the x converges to e to the x for every real x.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

   <figure xml:id="fig-skel-exp-taylor">
  <caption>Schematic graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and its Taylor polynomials
  <m>p_1, \dots, p_4</m> about <m>0</m>.</caption>
  <image width="80%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-exp-taylor">
      <diagram dimensions="(440,360)" margins="8">
        <!-- Schematic: e^x with Taylor polynomials p1..p4 about 0.
             No axis numbers; dashed markers show a representative x>0 with an
             intermediate c (0<c<x), and a representative x<0 with c (x<c<0). -->
        <definition>f(x) = exp(x)</definition>
        <definition>p1(x) = 1 + x</definition>
        <definition>p2(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2</definition>
        <definition>p3(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6</definition>
        <definition>p4(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-2.7, -1.9, 2.25, 8)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <graph at="p1" function="p1" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p2" function="p2" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="green" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p3" function="p3" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="purple" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p4" function="p4" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
          <label at="flabel" anchor="(-1.55, 4.4)" alignment="w" color="blue">
