            <annotation ref="fgraph" text="The function f of x equals e to the x, a thick 
            blue curve"/>
            <annotation ref="p1" text="The first degree Taylor polynomial p 1"/>
            <annotation ref="p2" text="The second degree Taylor polynomial p 2"/>
            <annotation ref="p3" text="The third degree Taylor polynomial p 3"/>
            <annotation ref="p4" text="The fourth degree Taylor polynomial p 4"/>
            <annotation ref="center" text="The center of expansion where the curves meet on the vertical axis"/>
            <annotation ref="pos-x" text="A dashed marker at a representative positive x"/>
            <annotation ref="pos-sample" text="A point at c between 0 and the positive x, where e to the c is less than e to the x"/>
            <annotation ref="neg-x" text="A dashed marker at a representative negative x"/>
            <annotation ref="neg-sample" text="A point at c between the negative x and 0, where e to the c is less than 1"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>

<p component="web">
The animation in <xref ref="fig-skel-exp-taylor-video"/> illustrates this
convergence geometrically: as <m>n</m> increases, the Taylor polynomials
<m>p_n</m> hug the graph of
<m>f(x) = e^x</m> over a wider and wider interval, matching the fact that
<m>R_n(x)\to 0</m> for every <m>x\in\R</m>.
</p>
<p component="print">
An animation of this convergence <mdash/> the Taylor polynomials
<m>p_n</m> hugging the graph of
<m>f(x) = e^x</m> over a wider and wider interval as <m>n</m> grows
<mdash/> accompanies the online version of these notes.
</p>

